{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 6,
  "estimated_minutes": 45,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The necessary background is a straightforward lessor–lessee royalty dispute, with Dorchester and Chesapeake seeking competing interpretations of paragraph 3(b) on cross-motions for summary judgment."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must understand actual sales proceeds, field versus downstream sales, permitted pipeline transportation deductions, and residue gas, but need not master the industry’s regulatory framework."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Understanding d03 requires reading paragraph 3(b) against the oil-royalty formula in 3(a), the payment deadline in 3(h), and the residue-gas cross-reference in 3(d)."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Lines 22–32 clearly find ambiguity rather than adopt either interpretation, but the reviewer must connect several criticisms of both readings and avoid treating those criticisms as a definitive royalty formula."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "high",
      "note": "D01 and d03 share the sentence-relationship analysis in line 23, while d02’s geographic ambiguity also supports d03, requiring careful separation of local sentence interaction, lexical uncertainty, and whole-contract coherence."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d03: The reviewer must connect the competing highest-price and transportation-protection readings to three other royalty provisions and understand why those comparisons do not resolve the ambiguity. It also substantially overlaps d01 and draws on d02, making its boundaries harder to recognise in a paraphrase.",
  "reason": "Assign this case to a reviewer comfortable with relational contract interpretation who can spend time mapping the royalty provisions. The central economic distinction is between a highest-obtainable-price royalty and an actual-proceeds royalty protected against disadvantageous downstream transportation costs. The opinion explains both positions clearly, but recognising the defects separately requires distinguishing overlapping levels of ambiguity. The reviewer should focus on paragraphs 3(a), 3(b), 3(d), and 3(h) and opinion lines 14–32; the unrelated interest, accounting, and discovery disputes do not need detailed review. The key limit is that the court found the royalty language ambiguous and reserved its ultimate meaning for trial."
}
